  "nameStory": "The Nude Mining Bee gets its rather cheeky name from its relatively hairless appearance compared to most other bees. While many of its mining bee cousins are fuzzy and well-coiffed, this species has much less body hair, giving it that 'nude' or 'naked' look that early entomologists couldn't help but notice.",
  "description": "The Nude Mining Bee is a solitary ground-nesting bee that's part of the large mining bee family. These medium-sized bees are less fuzzy than most other bees, with a relatively smooth, dark body that has a somewhat shiny appearance. Like other mining bees, females dig individual burrows in the ground where they raise their young, earning them their 'mining' name. They're important native pollinators that are active during warmer months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Body Hair",
      "description": "Noticeably less hairy than most other bees, with a relatively smooth, almost bare appearance that gives the bee its nude nickname",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Ground Behavior",
      "description": "Females can be seen digging small burrows in bare or sparsely vegetated soil, creating little mounds of excavated dirt",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Body Color",
      "description": "Dark-colored body with a somewhat metallic or shiny sheen, lacking the dense hair that would obscure the underlying color",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Medium-sized bee, larger than a sweat bee but smaller than a carpenter bee, with a robust build typical of mining bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Look for Nude Mining Bees in areas with exposed or lightly vegetated soil where females can easily excavate their nesting burrows. They prefer well-drained soils in sunny locations such as garden beds, trails, sandy areas, and the edges of lawns or fields. You might spot their small soil mounds near flowering plants where they forage for nectar and pollen.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults emerge and become active as temperatures warm, with females beginning to excavate nesting burrows in suitable soil.",
    "summer": "Peak activity period when adults are most commonly seen foraging on flowers and females are actively provisioning their underground nests with pollen.",
    "fall": "Adult activity winds down as the season progresses, with the next generation developing underground in their burrows.",
    "winter": "Adults are not active; the next generation overwinters as larvae or pupae in their underground chambers."
  },
  "funFact": "Despite being called 'nude,' these bees are actually excellent excavators that can dig burrows up to 8 inches deep, moving soil particles many times their own body weight to create perfect nurseries for their babies!",
  "confusionNotes": "The Nude Mining Bee's relatively hairless appearance makes it less likely to be confused with other fuzzy mining bees, but it might be mistaken for a small sweat bee. Remember: if you see it digging burrows in the ground, it's likely a mining bee, while sweat bees typically don't exhibit this behavior.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "The term 'mining bee' can be confusing since these bees don't actually mine anything valuable—they're simply excavating soil to create nests, much like a miner digs tunnels. The 'nude' name is just a scientific way of saying 'not very hairy' and doesn't mean the bee is actually without any covering.",
  "compareHints": "Among the many Andrena species in New England, the Nude Mining Bee stands out for its notably sparse body hair, while most of its relatives like the Hairy-banded Mining Bee have obvious fuzzy bands or patches. Its relatively smooth, shiny appearance is quite distinctive in a genus where most species are noticeably more hair-covered. This makes it one of the easier mining bees to distinguish once you know what to look for.",
